      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ben and I discussed this, and we\u0027re on board. I did point out a few minor fixes below. Also, there are two big notes/questions:\n\n1) Internally, Skia caches data for a single gamma value. If you intend to have surfaces with different text gamma values (perhaps because of multi-display?), then there could be some cache thrashing. Of course, if those will be separate processes, then it should be fine.\n\n2) Our understanding is that Windows used to (still does?) only apply the gamma settings to ClearType, not to simple Alpha_8 masks? Is that what you\u0027d expect/want? Note that Skia has an extra define for this today (`SK_GAMMA_APPLY_TO_A8`).",
      "revId": "7ae020ec92db6304d5b40142cfc3e7c344d8fb65",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8c6170dd_fa7160f7",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 20059
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-07T18:10:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) This is a great consideration, and I was not aware of how caching works. However, it shouldn\u0027t matter here, because this is a global setting (only the primary monitor settings are looked at, per the DirectWrite API\u0027s). Also, as you noted, any divergent settings would be in a different process anyways.\n\n2) Yeah, this isn\u0027t exactly like ClearType, but it brings the rendering closer to it. We\u0027ve been testing this in Edge for over a year now and gotten positive feedback from customers on it.",
      "parentUuid": "0a0f64f9_d8667243",
      "revId": "7ae020ec92db6304d5b40142cfc3e7c344d8fb65",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
